    # 0913: Giant Cell Arteritis Visual Loss (C)
    items.append(format_spec(
        "op-c-s3-0913", "Step 3", "Medicine", "Cardiovascular",
        "Giant Cell Arteritis Visual Symptoms High-Dose Pulse Methylprednisolone", 5,
        "A 74-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with a 3-week history of right-sided temporal headache, scalp tenderness when brushing her hair, and jaw ache while chewing steak. Over the past 6 hours, she has experienced two episodes of transient, painless monocular visual loss ('black curtain' falling over the right eye) lasting 5 to 10 minutes each, with residual blurring of vision in the inferior visual field of the right eye today. Physical examination reveals an indurated, tender, non-pulsatile right superficial temporal artery. Visual acuity is 20/100 in the right eye and 20/20 in the left eye; fundoscopic examination of the right eye demonstrates pale, edematous optic disc swelling consistent with anterior ischemic optic neuropathy (AION). Vital signs are normal. Point-of-care laboratory testing reveals ESR 98 mm/h and CRP 52 mg/L. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate management for this patient?",
        [
            ["Immediate intravenous methylprednisolone pulse therapy (500 to 1,000 mg daily for 3 days) followed by temporal artery biopsy", "This patient presents with GIANT CELL ARTERITIS (GCA / temporal arteritis) complicated by THREATENED PERMANENT VISION LOSS (amaurosis fugax, inferior visual field defect, and anterior ischemic optic neuropathy [AION]). Ischemic optic neuropathy in GCA is caused by vasculitic occlusion of the posterior ciliary arteries, leading to optic nerve infarction. Permanent vision loss occurs in up to 15-20% of GCA patients and, once established in one eye, can spread rapidly to the contralateral eye within hours to days. ACR and EULAR guidelines establish: (1) IMMEDIATE HIGH-DOSE GLUCOCORTICOID THERAPY: In patients presenting with VISUAL LOSS, AMAUROSIS FUGAX, OR DIPLOPIA, treatment must be initiated IMMEDIATELY with INTRAVENOUS METHYLPREDNISOLONE (500 to 1,000 mg IV daily for 3 consecutive days), followed by high-dose oral prednisone (1 mg/kg/day, maximum 60-80 mg daily). If no visual symptoms are present, oral prednisone 40-60 mg daily is sufficient; (2) DO NOT DELAY FOR BIOPSY: Glucocorticoids must NEVER BE DELAYED while arranging temporal artery biopsy or vascular ultrasound. Diagnostic histopathology of the temporal artery remains reliable for up to 2 weeks after glucocorticoid initiation; and (3) ADJUNCTIVE BIOLOGICS: Tocilizumab (an IL-6 receptor antagonist) can be added as a glucocorticoid-sparing agent for long-term maintenance, but high-dose corticosteroids are the immediate emergency standard."],
            ["Withholding systemic corticosteroids until unilateral temporal artery biopsy confirms granulomatous vasculitis", "Delaying corticosteroids while awaiting temporal artery biopsy leads to irreversible bilateral blindness in up to 50% of untreated patients."],
            ["Initiation of low-dose oral prednisone 20 mg daily with outpatient ophthalmology consultation in 48 hours", "Low-dose prednisone (20 mg) is adequate for polymyalgia rheumatica but completely fails to suppress the fulminant vasculitis of GCA with visual loss."],
            ["Emergency transcatheter ophthalmic artery thrombolysis with tissue plasminogen activator (tPA)", "Intra-arterial thrombolysis is ineffective and contraindicated in GCA because ischemia is driven by transmural arterial wall granulomatous inflammation, not acute embolic thrombus."],
            ["Initiation of subcutaneous tocilizumab monotherapy without systemic corticosteroids", "Tocilizumab monotherapy takes several days to achieve therapeutic immunosuppression and is contraindicated as emergency monotherapy for acute visual loss."]
        ],
        "GCA with visual symptoms (amaurosis fugax, AION) = Immediate IV methylprednisolone pulse (500-1000 mg/d x 3d) before biopsy (prevent bilateral blindness).",
        "Select immediate high-dose intravenous pulse methylprednisolone for giant cell arteritis with visual symptoms, recognizing that treatment must precede biopsy to prevent contralateral blindness.",
        "Withholding systemic corticosteroids until unilateral temporal artery biopsy confirms granulomatous vasculitis",
        "Biopsy confirmation must never delay treatment; temporal artery histology remains diagnostic for 2 weeks after starting steroids, while vision loss is irreversible.",
        refs['op-c-s3-0913']['citation']
    ))

    # 0914: BPH Combination Pharmacotherapy (D)
    items.append(format_spec(
        "op-c-s3-0914", "Step 3", "Medicine", "Renal",
        "Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia Combination Alpha-Blocker 5-ARI Therapy", 4,
        "A 68-year-old man presents with progressive lower urinary tract symptoms (LUTS) over the past 12 months. He reports weak urinary stream, hesitancy, terminal dribbling, sensation of incomplete emptying, and nocturia 4 to 5 times per night. His International Prostate Symptom Score (IPSS) is 24 (severe symptoms). He has no hematuria, dysuria, or fever. Digital rectal examination reveals a smooth, firm, symmetrical, and markedly enlarged prostate estimated at 55 grams, without nodules or tenderness. Serum prostate-specific antigen (PSA) is 3.8 ng/mL (reference <4.0 ng/mL), and serum creatinine is 1.0 mg/dL. Post-void residual bladder volume measured by ultrasound is 120 mL. He has never received pharmacotherapy for prostatic disease. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial medical pharmacotherapy for this patient?",
        [
            ["Combination therapy with an alpha-1 adrenergic antagonist (tamsulosin) and a 5-alpha reductase inhibitor (finasteride)", "This patient has SEVERE LOWER URINARY TRACT SYMPTOMS (IPSS 24) secondary to BENIGN PROSTATIC HYPERPLASIA (BPH) with high-risk features for disease progression: PROSTATE VOLUME >30-40 GRAMS (here 55 g) and PSA >= 1.5 ng/mL. The landmark MTOPS (Medical Therapy of Prostatic Symptoms) and CombAT clinical trials established that: (1) ALPHA-1 ADRENERGIC ANTAGONISTS (e.g., tamsulosin 0.4 mg daily): Relax smooth muscle in the prostatic stroma, bladder neck, and prostatic capsule, providing RAPID SYMPTOM RELIEF within days to weeks; BUT (2) Alpha-blockers do NOT alter prostate size or long-term disease progression; (3) 5-ALPHA REDUCTASE INHIBITORS (5-ARIs, e.g., finasteride 5 mg or dutasteride 0.5 mg daily): Inhibit the conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone (DHT), the primary androgen driving prostatic epithelial hyperplasia. Over 6 to 12 months, 5-ARIs REDUCE PROSTATE VOLUME BY 20% TO 25% and REDUCE THE LONG-TERM RISK OF ACUTE URINARY RETENTION AND BPH-RELATED SURGERY BY >50%; and (4) COMBINATION THERAPY (tamsulosin + finasteride): Is significantly superior to either monotherapy in patients with enlarged prostates (>30 g), providing both immediate symptom relief and long-term risk reduction. Monotherapy with an alpha-blocker alone leaves him at high risk of acute retention."],
            ["Monotherapy with an alpha-1 adrenergic antagonist (tamsulosin) indefinitely, avoiding 5-alpha reductase inhibitors", "Alpha-blocker monotherapy improves dynamic flow but does not reduce prostate volume or prevent long-term urinary retention in enlarged glands (>30 g)."],
            ["Monotherapy with finasteride 5 mg daily without an alpha-adrenergic antagonist", "Finasteride monotherapy requires 6 to 12 months to achieve clinical efficacy; omitting an alpha-blocker leaves the patient with severe untreated symptoms."],
            ["Immediate referral for transurethral resection of the prostate (TURP) prior to any medication trial", "Surgical intervention (TURP) is reserved for patients who fail medical combination therapy or develop refractory retention, renal insufficiency, or recurrent stones."],
            ["Initiation of daily oral oxybutynin 10 mg as monotherapy to reduce urinary frequency", "Anticholinergics (oxybutynin) reduce detrusor contractility and, if given as monotherapy in severe bladder outlet obstruction, precipitate acute urinary retention."]
        ],
        "Severe BPH with enlarged prostate (55g) = Combination alpha-blocker (tamsulosin) + 5-ARI (finasteride) to relieve symptoms and shrink prostate.",
        "Apply MTOPS/CombAT trial evidence: combine an alpha-1 antagonist (rapid symptom relief) with a 5-alpha reductase inhibitor (reduces prostate size and retention risk) for BPH with prostate >30g.",
        "Monotherapy with an alpha-1 adrenergic antagonist (tamsulosin) indefinitely, avoiding 5-alpha reductase inhibitors",
        "Alpha-blocker monotherapy fails to halt progressive glandular hyperplasia; combining with finasteride cuts the long-term risk of urinary retention and surgery by half.",
        refs['op-c-s3-0914']['citation']
    ))

    # 0915: Overactive Bladder in Geriatrics Mirabegron vs Anticholinergics (E)
    items.append(format_spec(
        "op-c-s3-0915", "Step 3", "Geriatrics", "Renal",
        "Overactive Bladder Geriatric Pharmacotherapy Mirabegron vs Anticholinergics", 4,
        "A 77-year-old woman presents with persistent urinary urgency, frequency (urinating 10 to 12 times during the day), and urge incontinence (involuntary leakage accompanied by an abrupt desire to void) for 9 months. She denies dysuria, hematuria, or fever. She has a history of mild cognitive impairment (Montreal Cognitive Assessment [MoCA] score 23/30), hypertension, and chronic constipation. Behavioral interventions, including bladder training, timed voiding, and pelvic floor muscle exercises, have been tried for 3 months with minimal improvement. Urinalysis is completely negative, and urine culture shows no growth. Post-void residual volume measured by bladder ultrasound is 35 mL. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in pharmacotherapy for her overactive bladder?",
        [
            ["Initiation of mirabegron 25 mg orally once daily, monitoring blood pressure", "This older patient has OVERACTIVE BLADDER (OAB) with urge incontinence refractory to first-line behavioral therapy. In geriatric patients, particularly those with BASELINE MILD COGNITIVE IMPAIRMENT OR DEMENTIA, the choice of second-line pharmacotherapy is critical: (1) ANTICHOLINERGIC / ANTIMUSCARINIC AGENTS (e.g., oxybutynin, tolterodine, solifenacin) have historically been widely prescribed for OAB by blocking M2/M3 receptors in the detrusor muscle. However, antimuscarinics cross the blood-brain barrier and block central M1 muscarinic receptors essential for memory and cholinergic neurotransmission. Large prospective cohort studies and the AGS Beers Criteria establish that ANTICHOLINERGICS DRAMATICALLY ACCELERATE COGNITIVE DECLINE, TRIPLE THE RISK OF DELIRIUM, AND SIGNIFICANTLY INCREASE LONG-TERM INCIDENCE OF DEMENTIA, while also exacerbating constipation and dry mouth; (2) BETA-3 ADRENERGIC RECEPTOR AGONISTS (MIRABEGRON, VIBEGRON): Stimulate beta-3 adrenergic receptors in the bladder detrusor, promoting detrusor relaxation during the storage phase to increase bladder capacity WITHOUT ANY ANTICHOLINERGIC ACTIVITY. Mirabegron has no adverse cognitive effects and does not cause dry mouth or constipation; and (3) The primary precaution with mirabegron is dose-dependent blood pressure elevation; baseline and follow-up blood pressure monitoring is mandatory."],
            ["Initiation of oral oxybutynin immediate-release 5 mg three times daily", "Oxybutynin has high anticholinergic burden, readily crosses the blood-brain barrier, worsens cognitive impairment, and accelerates dementia in elderly patients."],
            ["Initiation of oral solifenacin 10 mg daily as a cognitive-sparing anticholinergic alternative", "While solifenacin has modest M3 selectivity, all antimuscarinics contribute to cumulative anticholinergic burden and increase dementia risk in older adults."],
            ["Indwelling Foley catheter placement to completely eliminate incontinent episodes", "Chronic indwelling catheterization causes recurrent catheter-associated urinary tract infections, urosepsis, and bladder trauma, and is inappropriate for OAB."],
            ["Intravesical onabotulinumtoxinA injection into the detrusor muscle as initial second-line therapy", "Intravesical botulinum toxin is a third-line invasive intervention reserved for patients who fail or cannot tolerate oral beta-3 agonists."]
        ],
        "Overactive bladder in 77yo with cognitive impairment = Mirabegron (beta-3 agonist; avoids anticholinergic dementia risk of oxybutynin).",
        "Select a beta-3 adrenergic agonist (mirabegron) over antimuscarinics (oxybutynin) for overactive bladder in older adults with cognitive impairment to avoid anticholinergic neurocognitive toxicity.",
        "Initiation of oral oxybutynin immediate-release 5 mg three times daily",
        "Oxybutynin crosses the blood-brain barrier, worsens pre-existing cognitive impairment, exacerbates constipation, and is on the Beers Criteria list of inappropriate drugs.",
        refs['op-c-s3-0915']['citation']
    ))

    # 0916: Chronic Insomnia CBT-I (A)
    items.append(format_spec(
        "op-c-s3-0916", "Step 3", "Psychiatry", "Behavioral Health",
        "Chronic Insomnia First-Line Cognitive Behavioral Therapy vs Sedative Hypnotics", 4,
        "A 69-year-old retired schoolteacher presents with difficulty sleeping that has persisted for 8 months. She reports taking 60 to 90 minutes to fall asleep each night, waking up 3 to 4 times during the night with difficulty returning to sleep, and waking up at 4:30 AM feeling unrefreshed. She spends 9 to 10 hours in bed each night attempting to sleep. During the day, she feels fatigued, irritable, and worries incessantly about how poorly she will sleep the upcoming night. She has tried drinking chamomile tea and taking over-the-counter diphenhydramine 50 mg nightly, which produced daytime grogginess, dry mouth, and urinary hesitancy without improving her sleep. Medical history includes well-controlled hypertension. Vital signs, physical examination, and routine laboratory tests are normal. She requests a prescription for zolpidem to help her sleep. Which of the following is the most appropriate first-line management for this patient?",
        [
            ["Cognitive Behavioral Therapy for Insomnia (CBT-I) including stimulus control and sleep restriction therapy", "This patient meets clinical criteria for CHRONIC INSOMNIA DISORDER (sleep onset and/or maintenance difficulties occurring >= 3 nights per week for >= 3 months, causing significant daytime distress). Clinical practice guidelines from the American College of Physicians (ACP) and American Academy of Sleep Medicine (AASM) establish that: (1) COGNITIVE BEHAVIORAL THERAPY FOR INSOMNIA (CBT-I) IS THE FIRST-LINE TREATMENT FOR ALL ADULTS WITH CHRONIC INSOMNIA, SUPERIOR TO ALL PHARMACOTHERAPY. CBT-I is a multimodal intervention comprising: (a) Stimulus control therapy (using the bed only for sleep and intimacy; leaving the bedroom if not asleep within 20 minutes); (b) Sleep restriction therapy (limiting time in bed to match total actual sleep time, consolidating sleep and building homeostatic sleep drive); (c) Sleep hygiene education; and (d) Cognitive restructuring (addressing catastrophic beliefs and anxiety regarding sleep loss); and (2) SEDATIVE-HYPNOTICS (such as zolpidem, eszopiclone, or benzodiazepines) and ANTIHISTAMINES (diphenhydramine) ARE STRONGLY DISCOURAGED AS FIRST-LINE THERAPY, particularly in older adults (Beers Criteria). In the elderly, sedative-hypnotics cause next-day sedation, motor vehicle collisions, anterograde amnesia, complex sleep behaviors (sleep-walking), tolerance, dependency, and a >2-fold increased risk of FALLS AND HIP FRACTURES."],
            ["Prescribing oral zolpidem 10 mg at bedtime with a 6-month refill authorization", "Sedative-hypnotics (zolpidem) are contraindicated as first-line therapy in older adults due to severe risks of falls, cognitive impairment, and dependency."],
            ["Switching diphenhydramine to oral hydroxyzine 50 mg nightly for sedative-anxiolytic synergy", "Hydroxyzine is a potent first-generation antihistamine that adds severe anticholinergic burden (urinary retention, delirium, dry mouth) in elderly patients."],
            ["Prescribing oral clonazepam 1 mg nightly to achieve immediate sleep continuity", "Benzodiazepines have prolonged elimination half-lives in older adults, cause daytime sedation and ataxia, and carry severe risks of physical dependence."],
            ["Ordering an immediate in-laboratory overnight polysomnography (PSG) sleep study", "Polysomnography is indicated for suspected sleep apnea, periodic limb movement disorder, or narcolepsy, not for routine uncomplicated psychophysiological insomnia."]
        ],
        "Chronic insomnia >3 months in older adult = CBT-I (first-line; avoids sedative-hypnotic fall and fracture risks).",
        "Identify Cognitive Behavioral Therapy for Insomnia (CBT-I) as the guideline-directed first-line therapy for chronic insomnia, avoiding sedative-hypnotic drugs in older adults.",
        "Prescribing oral zolpidem 10 mg at bedtime with a 6-month refill authorization",
        "Zolpidem carries significant risks of falls, fractures, delirium, and dependency in the elderly; guidelines mandate CBT-I before any pharmacological trial.",
        refs['op-c-s3-0916']['citation']
    ))

    # 0917: Treatment-Resistant Depression Atypical Antipsychotic Augmentation (B)
